 A letter is any character that has a case, such as e or é. Any other character ends a word: a space, an underscore, a hyphen, a colon, a curly quote. A combining mark ends a word too, and so does a letter of a script without case, such as Chinese or Thai. The shipped keywords are English.\n\nA match counts as a whole word when the keyword characters just before and after it end a word. The start and the end of the keywords count as such an end. It counts as the start of a keyword when only the character before it ends a word. So man: beard, red_haired_man and t-man hold man as a whole word, and “here” holds here. An accented letter or an apostrophe stays inside its word, so café does not hold caf and o’clock does not hold o.\n\nOnly the keyword characters count, not the edges of the search text. So in man: beard the search text : beard follows the n of man, and it counts as inside a word.\n\nThe picker shows at most 1000 emojis, from the start of this list. filterEmojis rounds a fractional limit up, as before the fix. When more emojis match, as for a single letter such as e, the picker shows the first 1000 of the ranked list. With an empty search text the picker lists emojis.json in file order, up to 1000.\n\nAn emoji matches the search text when it matched before the fix. The fix changes the order of the list, and so it also changes which 1000 emojis show when more than 1000 match.","fretish":"when emoji_query_typed the emoji_picker shall immediately satisfy emoji_results_ranked"}},"component":"menu","id":"SW-REQ-261004-H41S","parent":"SYS-REQ-260922-6642","satisfies":["SYS-REQ-260922-6642"],"title":"The emoji picker lists the emojis whose keywords hold the search text, and Enter picks the first emoji of the list. The picker reads emojis.json as a JSON array; text that is not a JSON array gives an empty list. The picker trims the search text and ignores case. The list has three groups in this order. First come the emojis that hold the search text as whole words. Next come the emojis with a keyword that starts with the search text. Last come the emojis that hold the search text inside a keyword. Each group keeps the order of emojis.json. When the search text shows in more than one place, the best place decides the group. A word is a run of letters, digits and apostrophes (' or ’). A letter is any character that has a case, such as e or é. Any other character ends a word: a space, an underscore, a hyphen, a colon, a curly quote. A combining mark ends a word too, and so does a letter of a script without case, such as Chinese or Thai. The shipped keywords are English. A match counts as a whole word when the keyword characters just before and after it end a word. The start and the end of the keywords count as such an end. It counts as the start of a keyword when only the character before it ends a word. So man: beard, red_haired_man and t-man hold man as a whole word, and “here” holds here. An accented letter or an apostrophe stays inside its word, so café does not hold caf and o’clock does not hold o. Only the keyword characters count, not the edges of the search text. So in man: beard the search text : beard follows the n of man, and it counts as inside a word. The picker shows at most 1000 emojis, from the start of this list. filterEmojis rounds a fractional limit up, as before the fix. When more emojis match, as for a single letter such as e, the picker shows the first 1000 of the ranked list. With an empty search text the picker lists emojis.json in file order, up to 1000. An emoji matches the search text when it matched before the fix. The fix changes the order of the list, and so it also changes which 1000 emojis show when more than 1000 match."}],"intentEdits":1,"sources":["changeRequest.body","changeRequest.title","requirement:SW-REQ-261004-H41S"],"status":"declared","title":"Rank emoji search matches on whole words before matches inside words"},"nextDecision":{"disclosureIds":["compat_change_unapproved:cf-38dbca455384f2